C = {
"02-0700": ("API2 Broken authentication", "Any token works.",
"Some APIs accept any token: expired, edited, or signed by someone else.\n\nThe server reads the token but never verifies it. On every request, check four things: a valid signature, not expired, issued by you for this API, not revoked.\n\nThen test it with an expired token and an edited one. Both should fail.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #AppSec #Authentication",
"some APIs accept ANY token 🔓 OWASP API #2, and the 4 checks that fix it.\n#apisecurity #appsec #cybersecurity #webdev #techtok"),
"02-0900": ("NIS2 for software suppliers", "Your customer's law is now yours.",
"NIS2 is written for essential and important companies in the EU. If they are your customers, its supply-chain requirements land on you.\n\nExpect security questionnaires, incident-reporting clauses and requests for evidence. Get ready with a named security owner, an incident process that can notify customers within hours, and proof that you patch and control access.\n\n" + CTA + "\n\n#NIS2 #SupplyChainSecurity #Compliance #SecurityEngineering",
"selling software to EU companies? NIS2 is your problem too 🇪🇺 what they'll ask you for.\n#nis2 #compliance #cybersecurity #saas #techtok"),
"02-1300": ("JWT pitfalls", "The token signed itself.",
"Three JWT mistakes that keep showing up:\n\n1. Accepting whatever algorithm the token header asks for, including \"none\". Pin it on the server.\n2. Weak signing secrets that crack offline. Use long random keys or asymmetric keys.\n3. Tokens that never expire. Keep access tokens short-lived and refresh them.\n\nA JWT is only as strong as the code that verifies it.\n\n#JWT #APISecurity #AppSec #SecureCoding",
"the token that signed itself 🪪 3 JWT mistakes to check today.\n#jwt #apisecurity #webdev #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"02-1500": ("Career: developer to AppSec", "Developer to AppSec.",
"Developers already know how software is built. That is most of AppSec.\n\nThe honest path:\n1. Fix security bugs in code you own.\n2. Learn common attacks on practice apps, never production.\n3. Join code reviews and ask what an attacker would do.\n4. Write up what you learn, in public.\n\nA year of real fixes, reviews and write-ups is what hiring teams look for.\n\n" + CTA2 + "\n\n#AppSec #CyberSecurityCareers #SoftwareEngineering #CareerGrowth",
"developer → application security engineer. the honest path, in 4 steps 🛠️\n#cybersecurity #techcareers #appsec #softwareengineer #careertok"),
"02-1700": ("Rate limiting login, OTP and reset", "A million guesses a minute.",
"A six-digit code has a million possible values. Without a rate limit, a script can try them all before it expires.\n\nLimit attempts per account, not only per IP. Slow down or lock after a few failures. Expire codes after a few wrong tries. Alert on bursts of failures against one account.\n\nGuessing only works when guessing is free.\n\n#AppSec #Authentication #RateLimiting #APISecurity",
"a 6-digit code has 1,000,000 options. without rate limits, bots try them all 🤖\n#appsec #cybersecurity #webdev #authentication #techtok"),
"02-1900": ("Question: token storage", "Cookie or localStorage?",
"Where does your web app keep its tokens: an HttpOnly cookie or localStorage?\n\nWhich do you use, and why?",
"cookie or localStorage for tokens? pick one and defend it 👇\n#webdev #appsec #javascript #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"03-0700": ("API3 Broken object property level authorization", "The field you hid is in the JSON.",
"Hiding a field in the UI is not access control. The API response still carries it.\n\nOWASP API #3 covers both directions: reading properties you shouldn't see (another user's email, an internal score) and changing ones you shouldn't touch (a role, a price).\n\nDefine readable and writable fields per role, per endpoint, and enforce them server side.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #AppSec #Authorization",
"the field you hid in the app is still in the API response 👀 OWASP API #3.\n#apisecurity #appsec #cybersecurity #webdev #techtok"),
"03-0900": ("SOC 2 in plain words", "Your customer asked for SOC 2.",
"\"Can you send your SOC 2 report?\"\n\nSOC 2 is an independent audit of your security controls against the AICPA's Trust Services Criteria. Type I: controls are designed well, on one date. Type II: they operated effectively over a period, usually months. Most customers want Type II.\n\nStart where auditors start: access reviews, change management, monitoring and incident response.\n\n" + CTA + "\n\n#SOC2 #Compliance #SaaS #SecurityEngineering",
"a customer asked for your SOC 2 report. here's what that actually means 📋\n#soc2 #compliance #saas #cybersecurity #startups"),
"03-1300": ("Excessive data exposure", "Your API says too much.",
"The screen shows a name and a photo. The API returns the full user record.\n\nThat happens when an endpoint returns the database object and leaves filtering to the client. Anyone with the network tab sees the rest.\n\nShape each response on the server with only the fields the screen needs, then check your busiest endpoints in a proxy.\n\n#APISecurity #Privacy #AppSec #SecureCoding",
"your app shows a name. your API sends their whole profile 📤 fix it on the server.\n#apisecurity #privacy #webdev #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"03-1500": ("Career: reading code for security", "Read the auth first.",
"New codebase, security review due Friday. Don't read top to bottom.\n\n1. Authentication: how users prove who they are.\n2. Authorization: where the code checks what they can do.\n3. Every write of money or personal data.\n4. Every input from outside: uploads, webhooks, URLs.\n\nMost serious bugs live there.\n\n" + CTA2 + "\n\n#AppSec #CodeReview #SecureCoding #CyberSecurityCareers",
"reviewing code for security? don't start at line 1. start here 🔍\n#appsec #codereview #cybersecurity #softwareengineering #techtok"),
"03-1700": ("API4 Unrestricted resource consumption", "The bill is the attack.",
"Some attacks don't steal data. They send you the bill.\n\nEndpoints that send SMS, build reports or call a paid AI model can be triggered thousands of times. OWASP API #4.\n\nLimit everything that costs: requests per user, page and upload sizes, and spending caps with providers. Alert on unusual spend before the invoice does it for you.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #CloudCost #AppSec",
"the attack that doesn't steal data. it just sends you the bill 💸 OWASP API #4.\n#apisecurity #cloud #cybersecurity #startups #techtok"),
"03-1900": ("Question: fields the app never shows", "Fields the app never shows?",
"Open one API response from your app.\n\nHow many fields does it return that the screen never shows?",
"how many fields does your API return that the app never shows? check 👇\n#apisecurity #webdev #appsec #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"04-0700": ("API5 Broken function level authorization", "/admin works for users.",
"The admin page is hidden in the app. The admin endpoint behind it still answers a normal user.\n\nOWASP API #5. Attackers find these by guessing names, reading app code or watching traffic.\n\nCheck the caller's role on the server for every endpoint, deny new endpoints by default, and test admin endpoints with a normal user's token.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #Authorization #AppSec",
"normal user calls the admin endpoint… and it works 😬 OWASP API #5.\n#apisecurity #appsec #cybersecurity #webdev #techtok"),
"04-0900": ("EU Cyber Resilience Act", "Security becomes a product rule.",
"The EU Cyber Resilience Act turns security into a product requirement.\n\nSince 11 September 2026, manufacturers must report actively exploited vulnerabilities within 24 hours. From 11 December 2027: secure by default, security updates for the support period, and a way to report vulnerabilities.\n\nFor product teams: a vulnerability process, a component inventory (SBOM) and an update plan.\n\n" + CTA + "\n\n#CyberResilienceAct #ProductSecurity #Compliance #EU",
"selling software in the EU? security is becoming a legal product rule 🇪🇺 the Cyber Resilience Act.\n#cyberresilienceact #productsecurity #cybersecurity #compliance #techtok"),
"04-1300": ("Server-side role checks", "Hiding the button isn't security.",
"Hiding the delete button is good design. It is not security. Anyone can send the request the button would have sent.\n\nPut role checks on the server, on every action, in one place (middleware or a policy layer). Deny by default. Test with an admin account and a normal account sending the same requests.\n\n#AppSec #Authorization #SecureCoding #APISecurity",
"hiding the button ≠ security 🙈 where role checks really belong.\n#appsec #webdev #cybersecurity #softwareengineering #techtok"),
"04-1500": ("Career: security trade-offs with product", "Security, in PM language.",
"Product managers weigh security against everything else on the roadmap. Help them.\n\nDescribe risk as impact on customers, revenue or launch dates. Offer options: fix now, fix after launch with a guardrail, or accept the risk. Size the work honestly. Share good news too.\n\nThe engineer who helps product decide gets invited to planning, not just the post-mortem.\n\n" + CTA2 + "\n\n#ProductSecurity #SecurityLeadership #ProductManagement #AppSec",
"how to talk security with product managers (so they actually listen) 🤝\n#cybersecurity #productmanagement #techcareers #appsec #leadership"),
"04-1700": ("API6 Sensitive business flows", "The bot bought everything.",
"Sold out in eight seconds. To bots.\n\nOWASP API #6 has no code bug, just a valuable flow without limits: buying stock, booking slots, creating accounts, posting reviews.\n\nFind the flows that hurt when automated, then add friction: device and behaviour signals, per-account limits, queues for high-demand drops, checks before money or stock leaves.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #BotProtection #Ecommerce",
"sold out in 8 seconds… to bots 🤖👟 OWASP API #6.\n#apisecurity #ecommerce #cybersecurity #bots #techtok"),
"04-1900": ("Question: API security ownership", "Who owns API security?",
"Who owns API security in your team: backend developers, a security team, the platform team, or nobody in particular?\n\nOwnership decides what gets tested.",
"who owns API security in your team? be honest 👇\n#apisecurity #devsecops #cybersecurity #engineering #techtok"),
"05-0700": ("API7 Server-side request forgery", "Your server, their request.",
"\"Fetch the image at this URL.\" What if the URL points inside your network?\n\nSSRF (OWASP API #7) makes your server request what the attacker can't reach: internal admin panels, databases, cloud metadata.\n\nAllow-list destinations, block private and internal ranges, re-check after redirects, and give the fetcher as little network access as possible.\n\n#APISecurity #SSRF #OWASP #CloudSecurity",
"your server, their request 🎯 how SSRF works (OWASP API #7) and the fix.\n#ssrf #apisecurity #cybersecurity #cloudsecurity #techtok"),
"05-0900": ("Cloud metadata and SSRF", "One URL. Cloud keys.",
"One request to the metadata address can hand an attacker temporary cloud credentials, if your app has an SSRF bug. One of the best-known cloud breaches started this way.\n\nOn AWS, enforce IMDSv2 (session tokens a simple forged request can't get), keep each instance role minimal, and block metadata addresses in any code that fetches URLs.\n\n" + CTA + "\n\n#CloudSecurity #AWS #SSRF #SecurityEngineering",
"one URL → your cloud keys ☁️🔑 why IMDSv2 exists.\n#cloudsecurity #aws #ssrf #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"05-1300": ("File upload risks", "It was not an image.",
"\"Upload a profile picture.\" It isn't always a picture.\n\nCheck the real file type, not the name or the browser's claim. Set size limits. Rename files and store them separately from the app. Serve them from another domain. Never let an upload execute.\n\n#AppSec #FileUpload #WebSecurity #SecureCoding",
"the profile picture that wasn't a picture 🖼️ file upload checks.\n#appsec #webdev #cybersecurity #websecurity #techtok"),
"05-1500": ("Career: scoping a pentest", "Scope it right.",
"A pentest only finds what its scope allows. Scope it badly and you pay for a clean report on the wrong thing.\n\nStart with what would hurt most: money movement, customer data, admin access. Include APIs and mobile apps, test accounts for every role, clear limits and an emergency contact. Book the retest before the test starts.\n\n" + CTA2 + "\n\n#PenetrationTesting #AppSec #SecurityLeadership #StartupSecurity",
"how to scope a pentest so it finds what actually matters 🎯\n#pentest #cybersecurity #appsec #startups #techtok"),
"05-1700": ("API8 Security misconfiguration", "The error told them everything.",
"One error page named the framework, the database and the failing line.\n\nOWASP API #8: stack traces in production, permissive CORS, debug endpoints left on, missing headers, unreviewed defaults.\n\nGeneric errors for users, detailed logs for you, strict cross-origin rules, and configuration checks on every deploy.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #DevSecOps #AppSec",
"one error message told the attacker everything 🗣️ OWASP API #8.\n#apisecurity #devops #cybersecurity #webdev #techtok"),
"05-1900": ("Question: error responses", "What do your errors reveal?",
"Trigger an error in your API on purpose.\n\nWhat does the response reveal: a stack trace, a database name, a framework version, or a clean message?",
"break your own API on purpose. what does the error reveal? 👇\n#apisecurity #webdev #appsec #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"06-0700": ("API9 Improper inventory management", "v1 is still online.",
"Version 3 shipped last year. Version 1 is still online, still connected to production data, missing every fix since.\n\nOWASP API #9. Keep a living inventory of every API host and version with an owner and a retirement date, retire on schedule, and scan for hosts that aren't on the list.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #AssetManagement #DevSecOps",
"you shipped v3. v1 is still online 🧟 OWASP API #9.\n#apisecurity #devops #cybersecurity #webdev #techtok"),
"06-0900": ("ISO 27001 for engineers", "The auditor asks engineers this.",
"ISO 27001 auditors talk to engineers. Typical questions:\n\n- Who approved this production change, and where is the record?\n- When were access rights last reviewed?\n- How fast are critical vulnerabilities fixed?\n- When was a backup last restored?\n\nEach answer needs evidence. Build it into the normal workflow and audits stop being a scramble.\n\n" + CTA + "\n\n#ISO27001 #Compliance #DevSecOps #SecurityEngineering",
"what an ISO 27001 auditor will ask your engineers 📋 have your evidence ready.\n#iso27001 #compliance #cybersecurity #devops #techtok"),
"06-1300": ("Staging with production data", "Staging had real customers.",
"Staging often has weaker access control, shared passwords, debug mode and no monitoring. Copy production data into it and it becomes the easiest target you own.\n\nUse synthetic or masked data. If real data is truly needed, protect staging like production, and delete old copies on a schedule.\n\n#DataProtection #AppSec #DevOps #Privacy",
"the breach didn't come from production. it came from staging 🧪\n#dataprotection #devops #cybersecurity #privacy #techtok"),
"06-1500": ("Career: certifications vs skills", "Certs or skills?",
"Certs or skills? Both, for different reasons.\n\nCertifications get you past filters, especially at large and regulated employers. Skills get you through the interview and the job.\n\nPair one relevant certification with visible proof: write-ups, tools, reported findings. Hiring managers remember the candidate who walked them through a real bug.\n\n" + CTA2 + "\n\n#CyberSecurityCareers #Certifications #AppSec #CareerGrowth",
"certs or skills: what actually gets you hired in cybersecurity? 🎓🛠️\n#cybersecurity #techcareers #certifications #infosec #careertok"),
"06-1700": ("API10 Unsafe consumption of APIs", "You trusted their API.",
"You validate user input. Do you validate what partner APIs send back?\n\nOWASP API #10: third-party responses passed straight into databases, pages or payments. If the partner is compromised, so are you.\n\nTreat third-party data like user input, use TLS, follow redirects carefully, set timeouts, and limit what each integration can trigger.\n\n#APISecurity #OWASP #ThirdPartyRisk #AppSec",
"you validate user input. but do you validate your partners' APIs? 🤝⚠️ OWASP API #10.\n#apisecurity #appsec #cybersecurity #webdev #techtok"),
"06-1900": ("Question: most common OWASP API risk", "Which one do you see most?",
"Ten OWASP API risks this week.\n\nWhich one do you see most in real systems, and where?",
"10 OWASP API risks this week. which one do you see most? 👇\n#apisecurity #owasp #appsec #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"07-0700": ("GraphQL introspection and batching", "One query, every record.",
"GraphQL-specific risks:\n\n- Introspection hands out a map of the whole schema.\n- Batching packs thousands of guesses into one request, past rate limits.\n- Deep nesting exhausts the server.\n\nDisable introspection in production, limit depth and complexity, count batched operations against rate limits, and authorize every field.\n\n#GraphQL #APISecurity #AppSec #WebDev",
"one GraphQL query, every record 🕸️ introspection + batching, fixed.\n#graphql #apisecurity #webdev #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"07-0900": ("API security testing in CI", "Test it on every merge.",
"Your API changes daily. A yearly pentest sees it once.\n\nAutomate on every merge:\n1. Dependency and secret scanning.\n2. Spec checks for endpoints without auth.\n3. Authorization tests: same request, two users, one should fail.\n4. A scan of the running app in test.\n\nFail the build only on serious issues, and keep humans for logic flaws.\n\n" + CTA + "\n\n#DevSecOps #APISecurity #CICD #AppSec",
"your API changes daily. test it on every merge ✅ 4 checks to automate.\n#devsecops #apisecurity #cicd #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"07-1300": ("CORS in plain words", "CORS isn't a firewall.",
"CORS decides which websites a browser lets read your API's responses. Scripts, servers and attack tools ignore it. It is not access control.\n\nIt can still hurt: reflecting any origin with credentials allowed lets a malicious site read a logged-in user's data. Allow your own domains only, never a wildcard with credentials, and keep real authN/authZ behind it.\n\n#CORS #WebSecurity #APISecurity #AppSec",
"CORS is not a firewall 🧱 what it does (and doesn't) protect.\n#cors #webdev #websecurity #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"07-1500": ("Career: explaining a vulnerability", "Explain it to your CEO.",
"Your CEO doesn't need the vulnerability class. They need what could happen, how likely it is, and the cost of fixing it.\n\nOne sentence of impact (\"an attacker could download every customer's address\"), plain words on exploitability, the fix with cost and timeline, and a comparison to a risk they already know.\n\nClear explanations get budgets. Jargon gets postponed.\n\n" + CTA2 + "\n\n#SecurityLeadership #Communication #AppSec #CareerGrowth",
"how to explain a vulnerability to your CEO (no jargon) 🗣️\n#cybersecurity #leadership #techcareers #communication #careertok"),
"07-1700": ("Secrets in Git history", "Deleted file. Key still there.",
"Deleting the file doesn't delete the key. It's still in Git history.\n\nOnce a secret is committed, treat it as leaked: rotate first, clean history after. Then prevent the next one with pre-commit and pipeline secret scanning, a secrets manager, and a sweep of old repos, forks and mirrors.\n\n#SecretsManagement #DevSecOps #Git #AppSec",
"you deleted the file. the API key is still in git history 🔑\n#git #devsecops #cybersecurity #webdev #techtok"),
"07-1900": ("Question: last secret found", "Last secret you found?",
"What's the last secret you found where it shouldn't be: a repo, a chat, a log?\n\nNo names. Where was it, and how long had it been there?",
"last secret you found somewhere it shouldn't be? 🔑👇\n#cybersecurity #devsecops #infosec #git #techtok"),
"08-0700": ("API keys vs OAuth tokens", "Key or token?",
"API keys and OAuth tokens solve different problems.\n\nAn API key identifies an application: simple, long-lived, often broad. Fine for server-to-server calls you control. An OAuth token carries a user's delegated permission: scoped and short-lived. Use it when an app acts for a user.\n\nEither way: scope it, rotate it, and never ship a secret key in a mobile app.\n\n#APISecurity #OAuth #Authentication #AppSec",
"API key or OAuth token? when to use which 🔑🪪\n#apisecurity #oauth #webdev #cybersecurity #techtok"),
"08-0900": ("Third-party access risk", "The vendor had admin.",
"They didn't break into your company. They broke into the vendor with admin access to it.\n\nList every vendor with access to your systems or data. Give each the least access it needs, separate accounts and MFA. Review quarterly. Write breach notification into the contract.\n\n" + CTA + "\n\n#ThirdPartyRisk #SupplyChainSecurity #IdentitySecurity #SecurityEngineering",
"the attacker didn't hack you. they hacked your vendor 🚪\n#thirdpartyrisk #cybersecurity #supplychain #infosec #techtok"),
"08-1300": ("Logging the right events", "Log these three things.",
"\"What happened?\" is the first question in any incident. Logs answer it.\n\nAt minimum, log:\n1. Authentication: logins, failures, resets, new devices.\n2. Admin actions: role changes, exports, config changes.\n3. Money: payments, refunds, payouts, with who and when.\n\nStore them where attackers can't delete them, and never log passwords, tokens or full card numbers.\n\n#Logging #IncidentResponse #AppSec #DevSecOps",
"if you log nothing else, log these 3 things 📒\n#logging #cybersecurity #devops #incidentresponse #techtok"),
"08-1500": ("Career: security champions", "One champion per team.",
"One security engineer can't review every pull request for 200 developers.\n\nA champions programme: one interested developer per team, a few hours a month, training, a direct line to security and real decisions. They catch issues at design time and spread habits faster than policy.\n\nRecognise the work and count it toward promotion.\n\n" + CTA2 + "\n\n#SecurityChampions #DevSecOps #SecurityLeadership #AppSec",
"1 security engineer, 200 developers. here's how to scale 🏆\n#devsecops #cybersecurity #leadership #softwareengineering #techtok"),
"08-1700": ("Dependency risk", "One package. Every app.",
"Most of what you ship is other people's code. One compromised package reaches every app that installs it.\n\nKeep an SBOM, pin versions and commit lock files, scan on every build, be wary of new and little-known packages, and be able to answer \"which apps are affected?\" within an hour.\n\n#SupplyChainSecurity #SBOM #DevSecOps #AppSec",
"one package, every app 📦 how dependency attacks spread, and what to do.\n#supplychainsecurity #devsecops #cybersecurity #npm #techtok"),
"08-1900": ("Question: dependency count", "How many dependencies?",
"How many dependencies does your main app have, including transitive ones?\n\nCheck, then share the real number.",
"how many dependencies does your app really have? check, then tell me 👇\n#devsecops #webdev #cybersecurity #opensource #techtok"),
}
